================================================================================
THE STAMP ON THE READING  (2026-09-21, later the same day)
================================================================================

Found by reading a file listing, not by a gate: the new census file's UTC name was eight hours behind
its own local mtime, which made `actuals.read_at: "2026-09-21T21:49Z"` suspect. It was wrong. The
reading it described finished at 14:58:15Z (mtime of read-actual-spend-20260921.log, in UTC); 21:49Z
is local time on a UTC+8 machine wearing a Z, i.e. SEVEN HOURS IN THE FUTURE. Nothing caught it
because the only rule over the field was `if not str(...).strip()` -- and an impossible stamp is not
blank. This is `check_out_stamp()`'s defect, from three hours earlier in platform/build/, reproduced
by hand in a different file: the tell that the earlier fix was a VALIDATOR without a PRODUCER.

Fixed in that order, producer first:
  * tools/read_actual_spend.py  `utc_stamp(now=None)` -- the one place a reading's timestamp is made,
    clock-injectable so an arm about "in the future" cannot pass or fail by the runner's timezone.
  * `census_to_json()` writes it INTO the census beside the lines it describes, so the artifact and
    its date cannot be separated, and `census_from_json()` reads it back.
  * every run prints `meter read at ...` and, when cost_model.yaml publishes a DIFFERENT stamp, says
    so in the same line rather than trusting either.
  * estimate_cost.py `stamp_problems(stamp, now=None)` -- refuses a stamp that does not parse as an
    ISO UTC instant, and refuses one after the clock, naming how many hours ahead it is.

MUTATION CHECK -- 13 mutants over the new code, in three rounds. Method as before: full tree copy per
mutant into /tmp, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 so no .pyc serves the mutant, an assert that the anchor
appears exactly once so a mis-applied mutation cannot read as "killed", control run first.

  round 1   control 48 + 25 green          5 of 6 killed
    KILLED    utc_stamp formats the naive local fields instead of converting
    KILLED    census_from_json drops read_at and always returns None
    SURVIVED  --save-census writes the lines but not the stamp
    KILLED    stamp_problems never refuses a future stamp
    KILLED    stamp_problems accepts anything it cannot parse
    KILLED    the boundary slips: equal-to-now counts as future

  The survivor is the interesting one, and it convicted an arm rather than a gap. The writer lived
  inline in main()'s --save-census branch, reachable only by a live Cost Explorer read, so no arm
  could touch it -- while the arm that cited `feedback_two_readers_one_format` in its own docstring
  hand-wrote the JSON and therefore tested the reader against MY idea of the shape, not against the
  writer. Extracted to `census_to_json()`; the arm now round-trips through it.

  round 2   3 of 4 killed
    KILLED    census_to_json writes the lines but not the stamp        (round 1's survivor)
    SURVIVED  census_to_json writes the lines but not the Estimated days
    KILLED    census_to_json does not create its parent directory
    KILLED    census_to_json writes the usage type as the key, dropping the service

  Second survivor, second real defect -- and it is the SAME tri-state collapse one level in. The
  reader did `raw.get(CENSUS_ESTIMATED_KEY) or []`, so a wrapped census MISSING the field read as
  "the meter marked no day Estimated" -- a real answer this project publishes as a caveat -- instead
  of "this source cannot say". A writer that stopped writing the field was therefore invisible. The
  legacy shape had already been fixed for exactly this; the wrapped shape had not.

  round 3   3 of 3 killed
    KILLED    census_to_json omits the Estimated days                  (round 2's survivor)
    KILLED    the reader coerces an ABSENT estimated_days to []
    KILLED    the reader treats present-and-empty as 'cannot say'

  13 mutants, 11 killed on first sight, 2 survivors each fixed in the SOURCE and then killed.

THE STAMP WAS THEN WRONG A SECOND TIME, in a quieter way. Its replacement, "2026-09-21T15:32:19Z",
was correct UTC -- and derived by a human reading `date -u`, which is the same authorship the fix
was meant to remove, and it named a census file written BEFORE the field existed, so the artifact
could not corroborate it (feedback_correction_wrong_twice). Resolved by paying the meter once more
($0.17) for a reading that stamps itself:

  evidence/cost/census-20260921T155109Z.json     read_at: 2026-09-21T15:51:30Z  (in the file body)
  session-logs/read-actual-spend-20260921T155109Z.log       rc 1
  session-logs/read-actual-spend-20260921T155109Z-replay.log  rc 1, offline, free

cost_model.yaml's read_at is copied from that artifact, and the run prints a disagreement if they
ever part. Cumulative Cost Explorer spend for measuring this: ~$0.79 (79 requests at $0.01).

ONE OPEN PROBLEM, and rc 1 IS THE CORRECT OUTPUT.  Both readings fail on exactly one line:

  EBS:VolumeUsage.gp3: prorata accounts for 39 day(s) held and the meter now covers 40 day(s) from
  2026-08-12 to 2026-09-20. If the resource is still there it is still billing, and the ledger is
  1 day(s) stale -- deleting it is the fix, not editing this

The orphaned 40 GB volume (FUTURE-WORK item 44) is still attached to nothing and still billing, so
this check goes one day further out of date every day and CANNOT reach rc 0 until the volume is
deleted -- which is an AWS resource deletion and needs the user's authorisation. That is deliberate,
and it is also why this script is NOT wired into verify_phase0.sh: a gate that is permanently red
trains people to ignore the script it lives in. Read it as a standing invoice, not as breakage.


