THE DOCUMENTS THE MEASUREMENT FALSIFIED  (same session, same PR as the behaviour)
================================================================================

A measurement that does not reach the prose is a private fact. Four live documents stated this
project's spend and all four were wrong; each is corrected in the same change as the meter that
convicted it (feedback_behavior_change_needs_docs).

RECONNECT.md "Money spent so far" was a seven-row table of GUESSES set in the same typeface as
measurements, under the heading "Still under $2" -- wrong by 6.7x. Rewritten as estimate-vs-meter,
both columns kept, because what the estimates got wrong is the useful part:

  row                     estimated          measured     error
  volume                  <=$0.20            $4.0912      20.5x   (and the largest line in the project)
  EC2 t3.small            <=$0.40            $3.4969        8.7x   (rate and type right; HOURS wrong)
  Bedrock + AgentCore     <$0.50             $3.5081        7.0x   ("below CE's resolution" -- it isn't)
  Polly                   $0.72-$0.77        $2.2613        --     (one round vs cumulative; not an error)
  control plane           free               $0.0000        --     (the one row that held)
  S3 + CloudFront         "cents"            REFUSED        --     (account-shared; not separable)
  Cost Explorer itself    not anticipated    ~$0.79         --     (measuring costs money)

Three of the seven were `<=` or `<` bounds and ALL THREE were breached. That is the reusable lesson:
a ceiling printed beside a measurement reads as a measurement.

THREE OF MY OWN NEW ROWS WERE WRONG ON FIRST WRITE, and the check that caught them was reading the
ledger instead of the summary I was working from:

  - "it is a t3.large ($0.0832/h), a 4x error on the rate" -- FALSE. `provision.py:62` says
    `t3.small` and the ledger line is `BoxUsage:t3.small`. The rate and the type were both correct
    and the error was entirely in the HOURS: 168.122 h billed against an assumed <=19 h. The real
    finding is better than the one I invented -- 3 of those 8 days are a full-24-hour weekend with no
    artifact of any kind, $1.4976, 43% of the line, and the honest way to record idle spend is to
    claim it and name it.
  - "Bedrock ... $1.4805" -- FALSE, and sourced from nothing. Summed from the ledger: $3.1543
    Bedrock + $0.3538 AgentCore = $3.5081.
  - "S3 PUT + CloudFront ... $0.0412, now separately measured" -- FALSE in the strongest way: those
    lines are REFUSALS, not attributions. S3 is $1,855.19 + $519.72 + $2,007.82 account-wide over the
    window on all 51 days and the payload bucket's share is not separable at day granularity.
    CloudFront viewer egress is the one line with a real ceiling -- account-wide $0.0000 -- so
    serving the four videos is below the meter's own resolution. Publishing a dollar figure for a
    line the ledger explicitly declines to attribute would have undone the refusal.

WHITEPAPER.md:1156 said "under $2.15 in AWS charges" -- 6.2x low. Corrected in place rather than
footnoted, because the chapter it sits in is the cost chapter: the earlier figure was a sum of the
lines the cost model HAD, and the three families it lacked come to $9.85 between them.

cost_model.yaml's `ce_request_note` said "roughly $0.45 in total went on measuring this" while this
log counted 79 requests. Two readers, one fact, and the yaml was the stale one: it predates the
second census that the read_at work forced. Now states ~$0.79 with this file named as its source and
marked as a running total that only grows (feedback_perishable_claim_cannot_be_checked).

`provision.py:357` was cited twice (here and in cost_model.yaml) for a line that is at 358.

